A natural number $ n $ is **perfect** if the **sum of its proper divisors** (i.e., all divisors other than $ n $ itself) equals $ n $.

So, to show that $ 2^{30}(2^{31} - 1) $ is a perfect number, we need to compute the sum of its **proper divisors** and show it equals the number itself.

